    "description": "Glass in Iran has served domestic, commercial, and architectural purposes, from blown vessels to sheet glass and mirror decoration. Its history combines local production, imported objects, and industrial investment, with substantial uncertainty about the origins of some early surviving pieces. [s1][s2][s3]",
    "tags": ["Glass", "Building materials", "Mirrorwork", "Industrial history", "Architectural decoration"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T12:12:47Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T12:12:47Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Glass in Iran: Industry and Architecture | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Discover Iran's glassmaking history, the development of sheet-glass manufacture, and the architectural craft of mirror mosaics."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "objects-and-origins",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A material with several histories",
        "content": "Glass connects everyday objects with architectural surfaces. Iran's historical record includes bottles and drinking vessels, imported mirrors, and glass used in buildings. These uses should not be treated as evidence for a single, continuously documented industry: the origins of an excavated vessel, the movement of a luxury mirror, and factory production each pose different research questions. [s1][s2][s3]\n\nGlassblowing originated in the Syro-Palestinian region during the first century BCE and subsequently spread to neighboring regions. The Parthian period in Iranian history overlaps this expansion, but evidence for production within Persia remains limited. Finding glass within an imperial territory does not by itself establish that it was made locally; imports and local manufacture can be difficult to distinguish. [s1]\n\nLater evidence also records interaction with overseas suppliers. Venetian vessels and mirrors reached the Safavid court, while Bohemian glass entered Iran during the nineteenth century. Objects once broadly labeled Safavid in European collections have sometimes been reassigned to the later Qajar period. Such revisions explain why a confident stylistic label should not replace documented provenance. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "industrial-transition",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The transition to industrial manufacture",
        "content": "Willem Floor's industrial history describes a sector dominated by small craft workshops before mechanized expansion during the twentieth century. It records modern equipment in Tabriz in 1932 and a modern factory established in Tehran in 1938. These developments did not automatically secure efficient production: the account describes equipment operating below capacity and difficulties obtaining suitable soda ash, an important manufacturing input. [s2]\n\nFor building materials, sheet glass is especially relevant. Floor traces investment in sheet production and later development of float glass around 2000, including plants in Qazvin and Saveh. His survey also distinguishes architectural safety glass and double-glazed units from basic sheet production. This is useful historical evidence for industrial diversification, but its descriptions of market leadership, self-sufficiency, and future exports must remain tied to its publication period. They do not establish the industry's position in 2026. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "mirrorwork",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Mirrorwork as an architectural craft",
        "content": "Aina-kari is the covering of architectural surfaces with mirror-glass mosaics. Cut pieces can form patterned panels or follow complex surfaces, turning reflected light into part of the interior's appearance. It belongs to a history of building decoration as well as to glass manufacture. The origin of the glass and the place where a decorative composition was assembled need not be identical. [s3]\n\nHistorical travelers described European mirrors reaching Iran through long-distance trade. Mirror decoration subsequently appeared across royal buildings, private houses, pavilions, and other settings, with wide popularity in the Zand and Qajar periods. Its significance therefore extends beyond one building type. [s3]\n\nTaken together, these histories show why glass should be examined through objects, buildings, and production systems. A complete contemporary account still requires current factory documentation and comparable industrial statistics.",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      }
    ],
