    "description": "Iranian building traditions distinguish sun-dried earth blocks from kiln-fired bricks, using both in structural and decorative work. Historic Yazd demonstrates how earthen construction operates within a wider system of courtyards, vaults, shade, and water infrastructure, while modern research examines its seismic vulnerabilities and adaptations. [s1][s2][s3]",
    "tags": ["Bricks", "Adobe", "Yazd", "Earthen architecture", "Construction", "Conservation"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T12:12:47Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T12:12:47Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Bricks and Earthen Construction in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand sun-dried and fired bricks, decorative brickwork, Yazd's earthen buildings, and research on traditional seismic adaptations."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "two-material-traditions",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Sun-dried blocks and fired bricks",
        "content": "Two Persian terms clarify an important distinction: khesht denotes sun-dried earth blocks, while ajor denotes kiln-fired brick. Both belong to Iran's building history, but firing changes the material and introduces a different production stage. Historical accounts describe earth mixed with water and, in traditional preparation, straw or chaff before molding. [s1]\n\nIranica places early sun-dried bricks at Ganj Dareh near Kermanshah, with the dating expressed cautiously as possibly the late ninth or early eighth millennium BCE. It traces fired brick in monumental Iranian construction to the second millennium BCE. These are archaeological reference points, not evidence that every region adopted identical materials or techniques simultaneously. [s1]\n\nBrickwork also became a decorative medium. Builders varied bonding patterns, cut units, and projected or recessed surfaces to create shadow and texture. Glazed pieces later added color. A visible brick surface can therefore carry a decorative composition as well as reveal construction; its appearance alone does not explain the whole wall behind it. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "yazd-building-system",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Yazd: understanding the whole building",
        "content": "Historic Yazd, on the Iranian plateau, offers a particularly clear example of earthen architecture adapted to an arid environment. UNESCO's account describes earth used for walls and for vaulted and domed roofs. Thick walls operate alongside wind-catchers, sunken courtyards, underground spaces, and shaded passages. A qanat is an underground water-conveyance system; in Yazd, this infrastructure also helps explain the organization of neighborhoods. [s2]\n\nThe lesson is about relationships between components. UNESCO attributes the city's microclimate to a combination of building forms and urban arrangements, so it would be misleading to assign all thermal performance to the brick alone. Its heritage value likewise lies in inhabited districts and connected infrastructure, rather than a collection of isolated monumental facades. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "seismic-knowledge",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Traditional knowledge and seismic vulnerability",
        "content": "A 2022 study of Yazd's local seismic culture combined surveys, published evidence, and interviews with traditional builders and conservators. The researchers examined how construction details could influence earthquake behavior, using observations from the Bam earthquake to help assess the techniques. This approach treats traditional practice as knowledge that can be investigated rather than assuming that age alone proves structural reliability. [s3]\n\nThe study describes measures that reduce weight above vaults, including arrangements of small walls, voids, and secondary vaulting. It also discusses connections between structural elements. These details matter because unreinforced masonry can be vulnerable when heavy components and inadequate connections interact during shaking. The findings concern particular techniques and configurations; they are not a safety certification for every adobe building. [s3]\n\nThe historical record also documents industrial change. Floor identifies modern kiln construction in the early twentieth century, including a Hoffman kiln in south Tehran in 1935. That establishes a transition in production technology, but current kiln types, output, fuel use, and working conditions require new evidence. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s4"]
      }
    ],
